WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.000
This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. The Old Testament tells the story of King Saul, a ruler who was frequently gripped by waves of anguish and despair. When Saul was afflicted by such torments, his advisors didn't always call for a physician or a priest. Instead, they summoned a young shepherd named David, known for his skill at playing the liar. As the music began to play, King Saul's mind would settle and his mood would lift.

2
00:00:37.000 --> 00:01:10.000
For centuries, stories like this have been treated as legend, poetic, symbolic, even mystical. But do they point to something real? Today, a growing body of scientific research suggests that music, painting, dance, and storytelling, do far more than entertain us. They calm our nervous systems, reshape our emotions, and even influence our physical health. This week on Hidden Brain,

3
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:47.000
and in a companion story on Hidden Brain Plus, why the arts are no luxury, but rather a vital form of medicine for the mind. Think about the last time a piece of music stopped you in your tracks, or a novel kept you up late into the night, or a painting made you feel something you couldn't quite put into words. Why do these experiences feel so powerful? For a long time, scientists didn't have a clear answer.

4
00:01:47.000 --> 00:02:26.000
The arts seemed too subjective, too personal, to study in any systematic way. But that's starting to change. At University College London, Psychobiologist Daisy Fancourt has found that these moments are not just meaningful, they're measurable, and they can play a crucial role in our emotional and psychological well-being. Daisy Fancourt, welcome to Hidden Brain. It's lovely to be with you. Daisy, as a young woman, you were very involved in music, singing and playing the piano. What did you notice about the emotional and psychological effects that music and the arts had on you and your friends?

5
00:02:26.000 --> 00:03:18.000
I feel really lucky because I was born into a family of professional musicians, so the arts were just so much a part of my day-to-day upbringing. I don't think I really reflected on what a privilege that is to have that kind of exposure at such a young age. I mean, I remember so clearly that if ever I felt stressed or worried about anything as a teenager, I used to go and play the piano just sort of instinctively, and I knew that was something that would help me. And I certainly have noticed that a lot of the relationships, the friendships that I built early on, they came through playing music with friends being in orchestras or bands or being involved in musical theatre. So I think it was a lot more fundamental and foundational to me than I necessarily appreciated at that young age.

6
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:42.000
I understand, Daisy, that while you were in college, you began working in a pediatric hospital. Tell me about this work, where it was and what you did. It was an incredible program that was open to students at Oxford University where I was studying. And alongside your studies, you could train to go and deliver arts programs in hospitals. And it was something I just jumped at immediately because I was still fascinated by science and research.

7
00:03:42.000 --> 00:04:20.000
medicine and keen to find a way to blend the two. So we received training and then worked in pediatric hospital wards doing singing workshops with the children. And I was just completely blown away by the impact that I saw the singing having. I kind of expected it to be a nice bit of entertainment. But actually I was seeing directly that singing with children who were really anxious and crying and distressed would stop them crying and would actually completely calm them down. And I saw these amazing interactions between patients and their siblings that actually music was providing this form of emotional

8
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:46.000
and social connection for them, even in this very challenging and often inhumane kind of environments of the hospital. So I was actually able to witness the benefits I hadn't really appreciated that music could have. I understand that on one occasion you observed a premature baby in the hospital who was crying inconsolably and refusing to eat.

9
00:04:46.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Tell me that story. We were working in NICUS, the neonatal intensive care units, with some of the parents and actually trying to help the mothers sing with their babies. And I remember this one child who was just crying and crying, and the mother was really struggling, and you could see it was really impacting her psychologically as well. But as soon as we started singing this lullaby and she started singing along with her baby as well, the baby's behaviour just completely changed. And within a couple of minutes, it was suddenly feeding again.

10
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:57.000
I'm assuming that part of this might have also been that singing was kind of, the mom down, and perhaps that was also sending a signal to her child. Absolutely, and this is something that we actually saw so many times across different wards, that often the parents were the ones who were the most anxious and really actually struggling the most with the situations they were in, and actually giving them a way that they could engage and play and respond with their children through singing was something that was supporting that whole family unit together. Another time you saw a child being treated for burns, he was in severe pain.

11
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:22.000
What did you observe, Daisy? Well, after I finished college, I actually got a full-time job working in our National Health Service. So working in hospitals, designing new arts programs. And one of the wards we worked on was in the emergency departments. And I remember there was a little boy who'd come in who'd had really terrible burns. And there was a delay in managing to get morphine to him. And he was calling out for pain relief. But actually, there was a theatre group that we had in the hospital.

12
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:42.000
And they started doing this performance for him in the emergency department. And again, it was just this complete change. in his behaviour. He suddenly wasn't asking for pain relief. He was smiling and laughing along with what they were doing. And you could see that that distraction that had provided had been sufficient for him no longer to feel out of control in that pain situation.

13
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:57.000
And was this theatrical production? Was it aimed at children? Was it aimed at his age group? Yes, it was. It was from a wonderful group called Chicken Shed who specialised in how you could engage young people with the arts. And we brought them into the hospital. They'd specialized in working in disabilities.

14
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:45.000
art, but we brought them in and said, well, can we adapt this and develop programs that would be really targeting the children who are in the hospital? And they delivered some amazing programs across our pediatric wards. So even as you were seeing the effects that the arts were having on patients, you were also noticing that among some of your colleagues, doctors and administrators, they saw this as a form of entertainment, not as a form of therapy. Can you talk about this disconnect that you've experienced, Daisy? Well, it was a sort of a situation. where if people didn't know what it was and you started describing it, it was sort of described as, oh, yeah, the hospital entertainment program. But actually I noticed that as soon as we started

15
00:07:45.000 --> 00:08:21.000
working closely with clinicians on their wards, they actually really started to get it. They started to see this impact. Like we brought music onto the dementia wards. And immediately when we started playing these songs, there were these patients who couldn't remember their relatives, but were suddenly singing along word perfect. And you could see the clinicians suddenly looking up and noticing. and then we were having these great conversations where we were saying, well, how can we use this? How can we use this with the patients to support them whilst they're in hospital? So I was seeing these moments-to-moment transitions in how the arts were being perceived

16
00:08:21.000 --> 00:09:01.000
by the clinical staff as we started working more closely with them. But it was still at a stage where we didn't fully have the evidence to sort of say, well, that's because of these scientific studies. We were seeing the impact, but not always having those scientific papers to fall back on to explain it. So it really highlighted for me this gap in terms of scientific research in being able to make that case rather than having to demonstrate it physically all the time. Daisy had seen that engagement with the arts was healing people and making them whole. When we come back, we follow Daisy as she becomes a researcher.

17
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:49.000
She discovers why the arts have such striking effects and how to integrate them into the practice of medicine. You're listening to Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. After seeing firsthand the benefits of the arts in her own life and in the lives of her patients, Daisy Fancourt became a researcher. She started to generate hard scientific evidence for the benefits of the arts on our health. Over the past couple of decades, she and others have identified distinct ways in which the arts affect our bodies and our minds.

18
00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:27.000
Daisy, you say that engaging with the arts can elevate our mood and ease. increase our sense of well-being, not just in the moment, but over time. How is this happening? And do we understand the brain mechanisms behind this? It's actually one of the things people report the most when you ask them how they feel when they engage in the arts. They'll often say they feel happier. And we actually are really starting to understand more about the neuroscience of why this is. Because when we engage in the arts, we are essentially activating pleasure centres in the brain and reward networks like the dopaminergic system, which leads to the release of neurotransmitters like dopamine, which are involved in those

19
00:10:27.000 --> 00:11:04.000
feelings of happiness, of pleasure. And actually, we used to think that we just got these dopamine releases at the kind of peak moments of arts experiences, like if you're listening to a song, and that chorus comes back and you have that kind of yes moments. But we also now know that you get those in anticipation of those moments. It's the build-up to the return of that chorus. Or if you're reading a story, it's those moments when you're about to find out who the murderer is in a big detective novel, for example. And actually, that's why the arts are so effective at giving us these dopamine releases, because arts experiences are constantly about tension and resolution and anticipating what

20
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:38.000
might happen and seeing if we're right or having our expectations thwart it. So essentially, when we engage in the arts, we are giving our brains these repeated doses of dopamine. And that helps us to feel happier in the moment. But also over time, we see that the more regularly engaged we are, the higher the levels of our happiness that we have, the higher our life satisfaction. And particularly if we're getting involved in doing and making arts as well, it can affect so many other aspects of our well-being, like our self-esteem, our sense of meaning and purpose and autonomy in our lives.

21
00:11:38.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Now, the arts evoke all kinds of different emotions, so we might, you know, watch a horror movie and feel scared, or we might listen to heavy metal and feel pumped up. How is it that these experiences can also produce feelings of happiness and peace when, in some ways the experiences themselves might seem antithetical to happiness and peace. It's true. It feels like a paradox, doesn't it, that the idea you could watch a horror movie or read a really sad book, but somehow it's still good for your well-being. And the reason for this is that when we engage in arts in, for example, sad or scary or shocking arts experiences, we actually experience positive and negative emotions simultaneously.

22
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:56.000
So if you experience those sad, scary or shocking events in real life, it would just be probably deeply traumatic. But because we know it's art, our brain treats us a little bit differently. So we've got a little bit of an aesthetic distance between us and that art experience. And that distance means that we experience those emotions, but we don't have to respond to them. We don't have to change like the scary experience in our lives. We've not got to try and stop that fear. So actually we get to contemplate it. That means our brains get to rehearse how they might regulate their emotions or think about how we might respond in the real world.

23
00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:38.000
And that's actually something our brains love to do. And it's actually really good for us as well because our brains are prediction machines. So when we engage in the arts, we're actually helping to expose ourselves to new situations that can help us navigate complex experiences when they come up in our real lives. And also that those experiences, even like listening to sad songs when we're feeling sad, it can give us a feeling of kind of authentic emotional connection, the feeling that other people might have experienced similar emotions, and that can also support us psychologically. And perhaps this is why when people are really upset,

24
00:13:38.000 --> 00:14:09.000
sometimes they reach for sad songs, which would seem paradoxical, because you would think that people would want to listen to songs that are happy songs, but very often we are looking for music that matches our internal emotional barometers. Exactly. And actually sometimes it's through listening to, those lyrics and songs or those melodies that we can start to understand our own emotions better, and that means we can then process them and be able to cope with them better, too.

25
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:37.000
We had a guest on Hidden Brain recently, Colton Skriffner, who talked about the effects of watching horror movies or scary experiences, and he was studying the effects that these have on our minds. But as you're talking right now, I'm realizing that in some ways there's a parallel when we watch a scary movie about a shark or a monster of some kind, in some ways It's a form of exposure therapy. It is. You see this most clearly with young children who might want to read a ghost story over and over again

26
00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:58.000
because their brains are practicing, having that fear, managing that fear, being able to rationalise it. And we're often really drawn to those scary experiences. But there is a kind of caveat here, which is some people like me hate scary films. And that's because actually we're forgetting that it is art. When I watch a scary movie,

27
00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:38.000
I get so drawn into it, I kind of think it's real life. And therefore, I lose that distance and I just get scared. So that is a kind of caveat here is that we have to be appreciating those experiences for art. And it's the same way why sometimes if we've listened to a lot of really sad songs when we're feeling sad, again, we can get drawn in just a little bit too much and actually find that it's not supporting us in the right way. Talk about the role of the arts in helping us to regulate our emotion, Stacey. when we engage in the arts it can actually give our brain a kind of toolkit of ways of managing our emotions so some of the time it can be that if we are painting or dancing or singing or drumming

28
00:15:38.000 --> 00:16:24.000
then we're actually cathartically getting rid of our emotions we might be venting them for example if we're feeling angry or expressing those moods through our arts but there are also other times when actually it can be really helpful for us to distance ourselves from emotions in our lives If we're doing arts, it can give us a chance to be really mindful and just focus on the arts and give our brains a break from stresses or worries in our lives. Or it can even give us that chance to think differently to kind of reappraise what might be going on. Lots of people will have had the experience where you do something really creative and actually because you're calmer, because you're focused on the drawing you're doing or the dancing you're doing, you actually find yourself thinking a bit differently about the world. and by the time you finished, you actually think differently

29
00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:44.000
about the problem you might have had in the first place. So arts can be this amazing way of giving our brains lots of different ways that we can then contemplate or reassess or cope with challenges we might be facing. I understand that one study in the US induced negative emotions in people by asking them to think about a very sad event

30
00:16:44.000 --> 00:17:14.000
or showing them photographs of tragedies like funeral scenes or experiences of torture. and suffering, describe this experiment for me and what it found, Daisy? This is a really interesting study that said, well, if we give people kind of puzzles or we tell them to draw shapes and copy things, then will that help to regulate their emotions, to calm them down, for example, or to de-stress them in a better or worse way than if we actually let them do creative things?

31
00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:51.000
And what they actually found is if people had that creative freedom, like they could draw freely or they could write expressively about what they were feeling, they actually helped to regulate their emotions much better, much faster. So it really highlighted that the creativity in the arts is one of the things that can really support us when we're feeling stressed or when we're feeling worried. I understand that you've seen something of this phenomenon firsthand when it comes to your own daughter. Well, I've got young children, and as any parents to young children will be able to attest, like you see children really having to grapple with some big emotions and big experiences. And something I've noticed with my own kids is that if ever

32
00:17:51.000 --> 00:18:24.000
they're feeling worried about something or anxious about something, then just being able to hand them some paper and crayons, it's incredible how much they'll start to draw that. Sometimes it's sort of angry scribbling. Other times it's more cathartic drawing of calm shapes. And my children are only toddlers, so they're really young, but that just shows how innately young children are drawn to using the arts in a very organic way to support their emotions. And I think we can forget this as we become adults. We can forget that the arts can be such a powerful way

33
00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:46.000
of supporting our emotions. So I always think children are such an amazing example of this for us. You've studied the effects of group singing on patients dealing with cancer. Tell me about this work, Daisy.

34
00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:59.000
I've looked at the effect that the arts can have in lots of different patient groups where actually we might have a medical treatment for the illness, but we might not have a solution for thinking about holistically what that person is experiencing.

35
00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:21.000
The work that I've done in cancer hospitals in the UK, we've looked at having choirs for people, which have been a way of bringing people together in a social setting where they might be able to find some social support and companionship and people who understand what they're going through. But also singing is something that physiologically can relax them. We've discovered through the studies that when we sing, we reduce our levels of stress hormones,

36
00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:39.000
and we also reduce inflammation in our immune system, which is linked not just to our physical health, but also to feelings like depression, depressive sympathisms. And we've also found that people can experience a real sense of confidence through the choirs if they are learning this new skill of singing together. They're putting on performances.

37
00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:53.000
We found through our research that patients who'd been in the studies were saying that they'd felt that their self-esteem had taken a real hit when they'd been undergoing cancer treatment. But this was actually a way they were performing again. They were feeling more confident in their bodies, more confident socially,

38
00:19:53.000 --> 00:20:23.000
and it was helping them to navigate the psychological experience of cancer. I'm also reflecting on the fact that group singing involves singing in a group. So it's not just the music or the singing. It's probably helping patients connect with others. Very much so. And I think we see this across lots of different kinds of arts experiences. They can be this vehicle to us getting social interaction or physical activity in dance, for example, or cognitive stimulation. So things that we already know are really good for our health.

39
00:20:23.000 --> 00:21:07.000
So the arts organically bring lots of those ingredients together. But the arts also do bring extra things as well. They're a chance for more multisensory stimulation and creativity and an appreciation of aesthetics and the use of our imagination. And I think it's the fact that the arts can combine all of those things into one experience that makes them so powerful for our health. Now, of course, trying to express yourself in art requires a certain amount. of craft and control. Talk a little bit about how engaging in the arts can give us feelings of mastery, feelings of control.

40
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:53.000
This is something that many people can find challenging in their lives, is finding a way that they can feel that sense of mastery and control. And some people can get that through their work or through their home life, but there are plenty of people who don't actually get that, who can feel really disempowered in other aspects of their lives. But lots of studies have now shown that if we can engage in the arts and build artistic skills, even really simple things like learning to play a piece on the guitar or learning how to do a simple craft activity, like do some knitting or crocheting or embroidery. This can be a really good way of actually building our sense of ability, a sense that we can do something, our sense of mastery. And this is one of the really core components of our well-being. So it's a very important thing to make sure that we're cultivating.

41
00:21:53.000 --> 00:22:45.000
But it can also then help us to build our belief in ourselves, our feelings of our own self-efficacy or agency. And these are really important life skills that actually affect all of our other health behaviours too. If people start to feel a sense that they're good at something creative, they've built up that skill, then that can help them to feel more confident that they could try a different skill in their lives. So so many studies have shown that something as simple as joining a community arts group, for example, can lead to quite major life decisions like taking up another health behaviour or changing job or deciding to go out and find new friends, taking on challenges people haven't thought they could do before. You tell the story of a woman named Shea. Can you tell me her story, Daisy?

42
00:22:45.000 --> 00:23:16.000
Shea is an attorney who currently now lives in Houston, and she is someone who described to me that she had a really successful career as an attorney, but just wasn't feeling really fulfilled from that work. She described her life as being kind of grey rather than technicaler. And she was really concerned about this, particularly as her mother became very unwell with developed cancer. And her mother was saying to her, I really want you to find that sense of fulfilment in your life. But She remembered being really happy through singing experiences when she was younger.

43
00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:50.000
So she took this big leap and she signed up to a week-long music course at Berkeley College, a kind of unconventional vacation. And she described to me how this was a complete turning point in her life. that that course made her feel so happy, built that sense of mastery, really changed her whole outlook on herself and what she could do as well as the rest of her life. So after that, she decided that she was going to shift her attorney work and still carry on, but actually make much more space for music and her life. And she started doing more singer-songwriting work and even doing tours.

44
00:23:50.000 --> 00:24:37.000
And now, like years later, she says that she's completely transformed that sense of well-being in her life. She's managed to use music to sort of, compensate and balance out the things that she wasn't getting through her work. And I feel like Shea's story is such a universal one. There are so many people who feel like their jobs are not giving them that full sense of fulfillment. But actually, don't realize that something as simple as developing an artistic hobby on the side could completely change that sense of balance in their lives and as a result, their whole experience of their well-being. So given all these strands of research, I'm wondering if there's evidence that the arts can help to

45
00:24:37.000 --> 00:25:12.000
word of depression, Daisy? This is a really important question and one that started fascinating me about 10 years ago, because when you add together all of the evidence we've been talking about for the benefits, for the brain, for our well-being, it starts to make you think, well, might the arts actually support our overall psychological resilience? So perhaps we're more protected against the risk of developing conditions like depression. So I work as an epidemiologist, so my field of work involves using very large data sets, cohort studies that track tens of thousands of people over years and decades of their lives.

46
00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:53.000
And actually, a lot of these cohort studies, which I analyze in the US and in the UK, have looked at people's arts engagements. They've included lots of questions about their arts behaviours. So over the last 10 years, my team and I have been publishing dozens of papers actually looking at how arts engagement relates to the risk of developing depression over the following decades. And we found that people who engage regularly in the arts and have an artistic hobby actually have about half the risk of developing depression over the next 10 years. So you could say, well, are these people just healthier or wealthier or have other patterns of behaviours in their lives? But as epidemiologists, we use statistical methods to basically

47
00:25:53.000 --> 00:26:36.000
try and account for all the other factors that could explain this relationship. So demographics, behaviors, genetics, other aspects of people's lives or even their past histories. And we've actually seen that this relationship is independent of these factors. In other words, arts engagement seems to act as this risk-reducing factor against the risk of developing future mental illness. Is it clear which way the arrow of causation runs, though? You could imagine that someone who is not feeling depressed, somebody who's feeling generally happy with their lives, might be willing to take up a paintbrush or try their hand at a singing choir, and somebody who is depressed might stay away from those activities. Do we know that it's the arts that is reducing the risk of depression,

48
00:26:36.000 --> 00:27:10.000
or people with lower depression who might be more engaged with the arts? That is such an important question and it's one that we've really been trying to disentangle. When we've run our studies, you're right that we have seen some what we call bidirectional effects. It kind of goes both ways. But actually we've been saying, well, which way is strongest? And actually we tend to see that actually arts engagement has more of an influence on future mental health than the other way arounds. And actually there are lots of ways that we can also say, well, if you currently don't have depression, for example, then is your arts engagement going to be related to the future risk of depression?

49
00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:40.000
Therefore, we know that it's not simply your current depressive symptoms. And basically, through using lots of different statistical methods, we've actually been able to demonstrate that it is the arts engagement that is predicting future risk of depression, even when we've taken account of those bidirectional effects. I understand that some of this research has also looked at the idea that the arts might help us to hold off cognitive. decline. Explain this idea to me and tell me about the work you've done here.

50
00:27:40.000 --> 00:28:29.000
So when we engage in the arts, we're really giving our brains quite an effective whole brain workout. We're involving so many different regions of the brain that might be involved in the visual processing of sheet music, for example, or the motor response that's required, or the memory, or the emotions that are being activated. And what we've seen through a lot of studies now is that when people are regularly engaged in the arts, it actually supports their They develop increased cognitive functioning. And we can see this evident in neuroimaging studies that have shown increases in the size and connectivity between different brain regions when people engage regularly in the arts. But we can also see this over time. As people get older, those who've got more regular patterns of arts engagement actually have better preservation of their cognition.

51
00:28:29.000 --> 00:29:03.000
And in fact, they even tend to have younger brain ages. we can estimate someone's brain age based on how their brain is connecting and the patterns of connectivity across brain regions. And people who are amateurs or professionals, their brains actually are younger as they get older compared to their chronological age. So that's on average about five to seven years younger as they head into older adulthoods, their brains are. And actually we've seen that this is related as well to a delayed onset of the symptoms of dementia. Because with dementia, even if your brain is starting to accumulate,

52
00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:39.000
some of the plaques that are typical of conditions like Alzheimer's disease, people who've got higher levels of what we call cognitive reserve, they're actually able to compensate for longer, so their brains are able to adapt and deal with it so that they don't show the outside symptoms of dementia until later on. And what we seem to be finding through lots of different studies now is that arts engagement is helping to build that cognitive reserve so that people, even if they start to develop dementia, they can manage cognitively, better for longer. And in some ways, there's an analogy here with physical health.

53
00:29:39.000 --> 00:30:05.000
So, you know, if you're very healthy, if you're strong and you go through a period of decline, you have longer to go before you get incapacitated because you have, you know, more muscle to start with, more cardiovascular health to start with. And you're making the same case, really, for what happens in our minds. The more we practice certain things, the more we develop a craft, a skill, a mental ability, a cognitive ability. This gives us reserves that help us, you know, save off decline for longer periods of time?

54
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:36.000
That's essentially right as a metaphor. But there used to be a kind of craze about, well, could we do this through brain training apps, like games you would do on your phone that would build that cognitive function? But actually when the big studies of this came out, the results weren't that impressive because there was a kind of realization that those games are only practicing one cognitive function at a time. But this is where arts really has the upper hands, because when you're engaging in the arts, you've got so many different cognitive processes. that are being activated by that engagement,

55
00:30:36.000 --> 00:31:03.000
that whole brain workout that I spoke about. And that's why I think the arts is such an exciting opportunity for supporting our brains as we get older. Across many years of research, scientists like Daisy have been building evidence to prove that engagement with the arts can make a real difference. When we come back, how to bring the arts into our daily lives and reap all the riches they have to offer.

