60
00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:43.000
that you're able to put aside. And there can be really easy ways of doing this, even sort of simple swaps that you can have in your life. So, for example, one that I do is I have what I call a creative commute. So I can't, I live too far away to cycle or run to work. But when I'm on my train each day, I'm not allowed to scroll my phone. Instead, I use that time to sit and listen to music or to read my book. And that's a really easy way of making sure I get those minutes every single day.

61
00:33:43.000 --> 00:34:14.000
You see that it's important to focus on consistency. So rather than trying to get four hours and one day and nothing for the rest of the week, it's more helpful to get 20 minutes every day? If we're engaging regularly, it's much more likely to become a habit that sticks. So you're much more likely to find that you don't just cancel that one day and then realize it's been a couple of weeks since you did everything. But it also means you can be getting those benefits day-to-day. And lots of the immediate benefits people get from arts engagements

62
00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:43.000
for things like stress and for mood. And those are things that we can all benefit from having the little pick-me-ups. embedded in our day-to-day lives. And we also see that a lot of the evidence about the long-term benefits is really focused on sustainability of engagement. So having something that you do as a habit for months or years of your lives, if you really want to be supporting things like brain health, for example, as you get older, or the reduced risk of developing various mental and physical health conditions.

63
00:34:43.000 --> 00:35:28.000
So I think it's really great to find that daily dose that's achievable for you. You know, some people struggle to start an exercise routine and they find it helpful to join an exercise class or maybe to get a trainer. And in some ways, perhaps the same is true when it comes to the arts. I think it is true because some people are concerned that they're not artistic or creative enough or they're not good at something. They haven't got the right skills. So it can be really effective to start a hobby, an arts hobby, by joining a local group or by having a couple of lessons, a bit of instruction to get you. or even using things like YouTube, so you've got some videos or an online community for some support to get you going. And actually for things that involve a certain level of skill to be able to really get the most of them,

64
00:35:28.000 --> 00:36:03.000
so things like craft activities or learning musical instruments, literature now suggests that if you can do something for 50 hours, then actually you tend to have the level of skill that mean you can really fly with it on your own and really start to enjoy it and get into the flow of it. So that can be good for people to recognize that it might take a few weeks, to feel like they're really comfortable with an artistic hobby. So to give themselves the time to have those experiences and build that level of comfort.

65
00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:25.000
Intuitively, it seems to me that if you can do an arts activity with someone whom you like, a friend, it's a little bit like starting a workout routine with a friend. You're more likely to stick to it. Definitely. And actually, there are some great ways you can do this in your lives. So in the UK, actually, we found there are lots of venues like pubs or bars that are now doing crafts evenings

66
00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:47.000
so you can go alone, you can socialise with friends, but you can have that artistic focus. Or you can meet up with friends at a gallery or an exhibition as a way of having something to look at as well as that time to talk to each other. Something that I've heard of some couples doing now is instead of going for a date-night dinner, actually going to watch a show or to go to a gig so that they're actually embedding those creative experiences

67
00:36:47.000 --> 00:37:26.000
with the social experiences that they've already made the time for in their diaries. I want to go back to something that you mentioned a second earlier because I think this is a real barrier for many people. You know, people say I'm not musical or I can't draw to save my life. Talk about this barrier, the sense of shame that we have about our own artistic abilities, especially compared to what we might see on social media of other people posting their accomplishments. How this is in some ways a barrier to us jumping into the water and trying something ourselves? I think it's really a sign about how we as societies, sort of taken arts engagement and actually commodified it. We've sort of made it this object,

68
00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:58.000
this thing that you're supposed to be good at, or you have to feel like you've had particular training to be allowed to do it. But just look back at young children, because when you look at children, all children sing, they dance, they make up stories, they act things out, they constantly draw or craft. They're not worried about where they're good at it. They're really enjoying that experience. And all kids have got that innate creativity. So I think it's really helpful to draw on children as inspiration and remind ourselves that a lot of the health benefits of the arts actually come from doing it,

69
00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:32.000
not necessarily being good at it. You say that just as we might plan our meals out, you know, that on some days we're going to eat in and some days we're going to go to a restaurant, we can do the same thing when it comes to the arts, that we can actually plan our artistic calendars? I think that's a really great way of doing things. So that might be a case of seeing if there are some exhibitions

70
00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:49.000
or a festival coming up or there are films that you really want to see or that there are certain gigs or things you need to book tickets for. It's really great to put those things in the diary because as well as having the day-to-day daily dose of arts engagement that you might try and embed in your life,

71
00:38:49.000 --> 00:39:17.000
having things like that can bring really exciting novelty. They can give you different experiences that might give you feelings of awe or excitements. And they're also a really good way of making sure that we're just building those additional routines to increase that overall pattern of engagement that we get with the arts. You said something important a second ago, which is that it's one thing to consume arts, going to a festival or going to an exhibition, watching a movie. It's another thing to be

72
00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:58.000
producing art. Talk about the difference. Is there any evidence or research about the benefits of doing one or the other? Well, if we think back about what ingredients we're getting when we're engaging with the arts, if we're watching something, we're being exposed to lots of different stimuli. But if we're doing it ourselves, we're getting further types of stimuli, often tactile stimuli, for example, which people can find very therapeutic. But also if we're building our skills, that relates to those experiences of mastery that we've spoken about or a feeling of self-esteem or accomplishments that we can get through the arts as well. So a bit like the same way we try and have a variety of plants and vegetables in our diet, it can be really important to have a

73
00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:41.000
variety of arts experiences because they're all exposing us to different kinds of ingredients that have health benefits. And for some health conditions, it doesn't really matter whether you are receptively watching something versus actively participating. So for a lot of mental health outcomes, we actually see that both kinds of engagements have benefits, just in slightly different ways for us. But there are other experiences, other outcomes. So things like outcomes relating to mobility or to pain, for example, we're actually having that active ingredient of participating. is giving us additional sensory engagement and additional mechanisms that can actually support those health outcomes. You recommend regularly changing up the kinds of arts that we engage in.

74
00:40:41.000 --> 00:41:14.000
I'm wondering why this is, Daisy. If you love something, why not just stick to it? If you love something, then absolutely you can stick to it to a certain degree. But sometimes what people find is what we call the hedonic treadmill. If they're doing something that they enjoy, they can sometimes start to adapt to it. and it doesn't necessarily continue to bring exactly the same kinds of well-being benefits. So people actually will often organically shift things up. So, for example, if you're someone that's really into crafts and you've been really enjoying doing a certain kind of knitting, for example,

75
00:41:14.000 --> 00:42:14.000
you actually might find that you're setting yourself different kinds of challenges or different patterns that you're trying to learn. But sometimes people can still find that it's good to mix things up and bring in a completely different type of art's hobby. because that can be a great way of exposing their brains to novelty, to sparking their imagination, and to perhaps making sure that whatever behavioral routines they've got in their lives keep that freshness and excitement to them. I think many of us do engage with music or the arts, but we often do it as we're doing something else. So you're racing home from work, maybe you're on a train, you're playing some music, but you're also reading your email. It's one thing to listen to a beautiful piece of music with one ear and quite a very important.

76
00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:59.000
another to immerse ourselves completely in it. You talked a second ago about how when you are commuting home from work, you actually are devoting your full attention to the art that you're consuming. Talk about this, the importance of actually sitting and savoring and focusing on the art, not just doing it with one ear or one eye. Yes. When we listen to music, for example, quite in the background, it can still be having some benefits for us, like reducing stress response, for example. But if it's not getting our full attention, we're not going to be getting the maximum impact from it. So I think it's really important that we are consciously aware about how much we are passively engaging in the arts versus giving it that full attention. Because when we give it

77
00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:39.000
full attention, all of our emotional responses are heightened, our stress responses are heightened as well. And also we've got a chance to really get into the flow of what we're doing. And if we can really get into that flow state, that feeling of complete absorption, where we're, forgetting everything else in our lives, it has profound benefits for our mental health. And it also has profound benefits for our brain health. It actually leads to synchronization between particular brain regions and enhances our overall attention as well. So I think it's really great that we can ensure that we are giving our time and space rather than just always relegating it to background passive consumption. I'm thinking, Daisy, about how sometimes when I'm

78
00:43:39.000 --> 00:44:14.000
eating, you know, some dessert, if I actually take my time with it and linger over it and savour it, The dessert tastes two or three times as good as a dessert that I'm rushing through. And the same thing in some ways is true for art. Absolutely. We get a much deeper appreciation and enjoyment from it. Essentially, all of our senses are heightened when we're giving ourselves that full attention on what we're doing. And we see things like increased dopamine release or increased feelings of happiness if we're really focusing on the art. A reminder, do you have a personal story about the effects of the arts in your life or the life of a family member?

79
00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:51.000
If you have a story you are willing to share with a hidden brain audience or a question or comment about this episode, please find a very quiet room and record a voice memo on your phone. Two or three minutes is plenty. Email it to us at Feedback at Hiddenbrain.org using the subject line, arts. Again, that's Feedback at hiddenbrain.org. Daisy, I understand that you have come up with a number of ways to incorporate the arts into your own daily life. paint a picture for me of what you've done? One of the things that I've done in my life is I've tried to work out across the day, when am I most likely to be able to benefit from the arts?

80
00:44:51.000 --> 00:45:33.000
So I've often found that if I swap my alarm on my phone to a really calming piece of music in the mornings, that really subtle shift can completely change my mood when I first get up and how I'm starting my day. I've also found some of the times like in the afternoon and sometimes I have a bit of an energy dip, where I know that actually putting on some music can be a really great way of, freshening up my mood or it can be a great chance to actually take a little bit of a break from work and just help me to shake up that slump in the afternoon. I'm somebody that really loves to be sociable in the evenings. So I've really enjoyed being able to find ways of combining my socialising with also seeing friends as well. That's felt like a really nice way of not having to

81
00:45:33.000 --> 00:46:09.000
find more time, but being able to maximize the time that I do have to have those more meaningful experiences with people. And I've also deliberately across my week, a couple of sessions in where I do things like a book club or I'll go to go and try a new class or a taster session of something because I find that that's another great way of keeping my engagement really novel and fresh. I'm wondering when you socialize with friends, you sometimes say that you skip having drinks or dinners and try and swap in an arts activity instead. Can you paint me a picture of a time when you did this with some friends, Daisy?

82
00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:46.000
I found recently that I've been meeting some friends at galleries or exhibitions that have been on. And it's a really great way to be catching up, but whilst you're looking around and able to think about and talk about the paintings as well. I also think it can be a really nice way. If you've got things you want to talk about with friends that are a little bit harder or more emotionally intense, to actually do something that's not just sitting opposite people at a dining table, for example, but actually somewhere where you've got a little bit of space and walking around galleries or listening to music, might be being performed in kind of public events, it can be a great way of actually giving yourself a little bit more time and focus

83
00:46:46.000 --> 00:47:27.000
to actually have some more difficult conversations too. I was talking to a friend recently who told me that, you know, before she had kids, you know, she and her husband would go ballroom dancing multiple times a week. And of course, I'm reflecting now as I'm hearing you speak that in some ways, that has so many different components of what you just talked about. You're listening to the music, you're dancing, you're social, you're being with someone. All of these things in some ways have profound benefits for us.

84
00:47:27.000 --> 00:48:12.000
They do, and they're often much more memorable as events to do together than simply going out for a meal. And actually, we do know from a social bonding perspective that if we do arts with people, so if we sing or we dance with people, we actually become emotionally closer with them faster than if we just talk or if we exercise with them. So it's a really lovely thing to do as well if people are forming new relationships or new friendships because it can be an amazing icebreaker and a way of helping to facilitate the development of deeper connection.

85
00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:36.000
